course: arts
lesson: 23
title: Animation: flipbooks, stop-motion, computer cartoons
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Animation is a lovely trick on your brain: lots of still pictures, each a tiny bit different, are shown so fast that your eyes and brain blend them into movement.
explain: Draw a stick man in the corner of every page of a notebook, moving his arm a little each time, then flick the pages: he waves, though no single drawing moves at all.
explain: Cartoons are made in three main ways: drawn by hand, made from models moved a tiny bit between photos (stop-motion), or built and moved inside a computer.
explain: All three are slow: one second of cartoon can need a dozen or more pictures, so a few seconds can take an animator a whole day.
explain: People say it their own way: "class" or "proper class" means brilliant in Ireland and Britain, "ya muppet" is a cheeky "you silly", and "jittery" means shaky and jumpy.
fact: A flipbook shows how animation works: still pictures shown quickly look like movement.
fact: Cartoons often use 12 to 24 pictures for every second of film; each single still picture is called a frame.
fact: Stop-motion means moving models a tiny bit, taking a photo, and repeating; Wallace and Gromit are made this way with plasticine by the British studio Aardman.
fact: Classic cartoons were drawn by hand, often on clear sheets called cels.
fact: Toy Story, made by Pixar in 1995, was the first full-length film made entirely by computer animation.
fact: Even with computers, animators still decide every movement; the computer does not make the art by itself.
fact: Studio Ghibli, a Japanese studio co-founded by Hayao Miyazaki, is famous for hand-drawn films such as My Neighbor Totoro and Spirited Away, which won an Oscar.
fact: Stop-motion looks jumpy if the camera moves, the light changes or the table is bumped, so animators fix the camera on a stand called a tripod.
fact: Many TV cartoons are written in one country and drawn in another, often in South Korea, Japan or the Philippines.
fact: Ukraine has a long cartoon history: a Kyiv studio made the silly Cossacks cartoons from the 1960s to the 1990s.
fact: Many cartoon characters, like Mickey Mouse and the Simpsons, have four fingers because it is quicker to draw and looks clearer.
fact: Cartoons break the rules of physics on purpose to be funny, like running off a cliff and falling only after looking down.
exercise: Why does Tilly's stick man seem to wave when she flicks the pages? | Still pictures shown quickly blend into movement | The ink moves around on the paper | The pages push the drawing along | Her eyes are playing a trick that only works on paper
exercise: How are Wallace and Gromit made? | Plasticine models moved bit by bit between photos | Drawn on sheets of paper with felt pens, then coloured in by hand | Filmed with a real dog and a real man dressed up in costumes | Made on a computer in a day
exercise: What is the difference between old Disney cartoons and Toy Story? | Old ones drawn by hand, Toy Story on computers | Old ones were made on computers, and Toy Story was drawn by hand | Both were made with plasticine models moved a tiny bit at a time | There is no difference
exercise: Which was the first full-length film made entirely by computer animation? | Toy Story | Shrek | Snow White | My Neighbor Totoro
exercise: Saoirse loves Spirited Away. How was it made? | Mostly drawn by hand at Studio Ghibli | With plasticine models in a British studio, moved bit by bit | Filmed with real actors in costumes and face paint in Japan | Made only by a computer program, with no artists working on it
exercise: Harry gives up because his cartoon "barely moves". What is true? | A few seconds can take a whole day | Cartoons are quick to make, so he must be doing it all wrong | One single drawing is enough for a whole minute of cartoon | Computers do all the work
exercise: Nina's Lego film is jittery. What helps most? | A still camera and small moves | Move the figures a lot between photos so the action looks faster | Take the photos while walking slowly around the table | Turn the lights on and off between photos to keep it fresh
exercise: Leo's cartoon was drawn in South Korea. Why is that normal? | Teams in many countries make cartoons | South Korea makes every single cartoon in the whole world | American cartoons are not allowed to be drawn in America | It is an illegal copy
exercise: What were Ukraine's Cossacks cartoons famous for? | Silly physical comedy, made in Kyiv | Being the first computer cartoons | Being made with plasticine in England | Having no pictures, only sound
exercise: Why do many cartoon characters have four fingers? | It is quicker to draw and looks clearer | Animators forgot to count the fingers | Four fingers is a rule from the government | Real animals all have four fingers
exercise: A cartoon character falls only after looking down. Is that real physics? | No, cartoons break physics for laughs | Yes, you only fall when you look down | Yes, but only off very high cliffs | No, because cartoon people weigh nothing
exercise: What is a frame in animation? | One still picture | One whole cartoon episode | The border around the TV | The person who draws
exercise: What is a cel? | A clear sheet with a drawing on it | A small battery for a camera | A part of a computer | A piece of plasticine
exercise: Do computers make cartoons all by themselves? | No, animators decide every move | Yes, you just press a button and the cartoon appears | Yes, but only for short cartoons of a minute or less | No, because computers are not able to show cartoons
exercise: How many pictures do cartoons often use for one second? | About 12 to 24 | Exactly one | About 500 | About 2
exercise: What is stop-motion? | Moving models and photographing each step | Stopping a film in the middle so people can buy snacks | A cartoon where nothing moves, only the sound changes | Drawing on a tablet
test: Your mate says, "Cartoons are easy, you just draw once and the computer moves it." What is the best answer? | Animators decide every move, slowly | He is right, the whole thing takes only a few minutes | Only stop-motion needs any work, the rest is automatic | Cartoons are really filmed with real actors in costumes
test: What would happen if Nina bumped the table between every photo? | It would look jumpy | Her film would look much smoother and more like a real cartoon | Nothing at all, because the app fixes every bump by itself | The Lego figures would vanish from some of the photos
test: Kid in Texas says, "Shrek was the first computer movie, no cap" ("no cap" means honestly). What is the correction? | Toy Story came first, in 1995 | He is right, Shrek was first | The first one was Wallace and Gromit | Computer films began in the 1950s
test: A cartoon cat runs off a cliff and hangs in the air. Your little brother wants to "try it off the shed". What do you say? | No way, cartoon physics is not real, you would just fall | Yes, as long as you don't look down | Only if you run fast enough | Yes, but jump onto grass
test: Leo's mum says his cartoon was animated abroad. Which is the best conclusion? | Cartoons are often made across countries | The cartoon must be a fake copy of the real American one | Only cartoons made in America count as real cartoons | One person makes each one
test: Granda in Cork reckons hand-drawn cartoons are "old hat, sure nobody does that now" ("old hat" means out of date). Which is true? | Ghibli is famous for hand-drawn films | He is right, no film anywhere is drawn by hand any more | Hand drawing was banned in studios when computers came in | Hand-drawn films are not allowed to win any big film prizes
test: Which is the best first try for a kid making a cartoon? | A very short animation, like a few seconds | A two-hour film in one weekend | A whole series in one day | Nothing, kids cannot animate
test: Ryan says the four fingers are a mistake. Which is true? | It is a choice that saves time and looks neater | Animators really forgot one finger | Cartoon characters lost a finger in an accident | Five fingers cannot be drawn on screens
test: Your Gen Z cousin says Wallace and Gromit is "lowkey a flex" ("lowkey" means kind of, "a flex" means showing off skill). What makes it so impressive? | Every tiny move is photographed by hand | It was made with one button on a computer in an afternoon | It was filmed in one quick take with the models on strings | The dog is real
test: How is a flipbook like a film? | Both show still pictures fast to look like movement | Both need electricity to work | Both are made of plasticine | Both show one picture only
test: Olha's mum loved the Cossacks cartoons. Which shows they are a fact of history? | They were made in Kyiv, 1960s to 1990s | They were made in America last year by a big film company | They were drawn by the Disney studio in Japan in the 1930s | They were made by Pixar
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a succession of still drawings is perceived as continuous motion. | The eye and brain blend images presented in rapid succession | The ink of each drawing is displaced upon the page | The pages themselves undergo movement of the figures | The drawings are altered by the viewer's touch
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the film "Toy Story" released in 1995? | The first full-length wholly computer-animated film | It was produced entirely by means of plasticine models photographed in succession | It was drawn exclusively by hand upon transparent cels in the traditional manner | The first film with sound
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the depiction of four fingers upon cartoon characters. | It is a deliberate simplification that economises on labour | It is an error repeated by successive animators | It is required by the regulations of broadcasting | It reflects the anatomy of every animal depicted
exam: Ascertain the measure most appropriate for reducing unwanted jitter in stop-motion animation. | A fixed tripod and small movements | The movement of the camera to a new position between successive photographs | The variation of the lighting between successive photographs to add interest | The enlargement of each movement of the figures between photographs
